    "description": "Telecommunications and internet access in Iran involve mobile operators, fixed-access providers, and the networks linking users to domestic and international services. Coverage, subscriptions, actual use, and reachability describe different aspects of this system.",
    "tags": ["Telecommunications", "Internet access", "Mobile networks", "Fixed broadband", "Connectivity"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T09:12:09Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T09:12:09Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Telecommunications & Internet in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand Iran's telecommunications through access networks, connectivity indicators, domestic and international reachability, and evidence limitations."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "access-and-networks",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Access is one part of the system",
        "content": "An introduction to telecommunications needs to distinguish the connection reaching a user from the wider networks carrying that user's traffic. Mobile and fixed access are entry points; access to a particular domestic or international service depends on more than the presence of that connection. Research on Iranian internet architecture describes international transit gateways and a domestically oriented National Information Network. The latter is commonly abbreviated NIN. [s1]\n\nThis directory separates operator profiles from infrastructure topics. MCI, Irancell, Rightel, and the Telecommunication Company of Iran have individual pages, while fixed internet providers and fiber-optic infrastructure have their own branches. These links identify the scope for further exploration; they do not imply equivalent ownership, services, geographic reach, or market shares.",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "measures-and-definitions",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading connectivity statistics",
        "content": "The International Telecommunication Union's ICT Development Index uses distinct indicators for internet use, household access, mobile broadband subscriptions, network coverage, traffic, and affordability. These measures should retain their separate meanings. A subscription count describes connections rather than unique people; coverage concerns the potential availability of a network, rather than proof of successful use at every covered location. The report's methodological framework is essential context for interpreting national comparisons. [s2]\n\nThe World Bank publishes an Iran series for individuals using the internet, identifying the ITU's World Telecommunication/ICT Indicators Database as its source. The inspected page presents a historical series extending through 2024. However, the underlying annual observations and their status were not successfully retrieved during this research, so they are not reproduced here. A chart assembled from partially inspected values would obscure rather than clarify the evidence. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reachability-and-restrictions",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Domestic and international reachability",
        "content": "Freedom House's 2025 assessment describes extensive filtering and pressure toward domestic services. It also reports a major shutdown during the June 2025 conflict. This is an independent rights assessment, distinct from an operator's technical description or an official statement of policy. Its principal reporting period ends in May 2025, although it discusses the subsequent June shutdown. [s4]\n\nLater technical research examines Iranian shutdowns in 2026 using several measurement methods. Its central finding is methodological: a network can remain visible in global routing information while actual traffic is disrupted. Conversely, apparently reachable infrastructure can be misleading when address location and routing paths are not checked. The authors explicitly state that their external measurements do not observe domestic NIN connectivity. Their results therefore cannot establish that all domestic services remained available. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "assessment-method",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "Separate questions in a connectivity assessment",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[\"Assess telecommunications\"] -->|\"Examine access\"| B[\"Network coverage\"]\n  A -->|\"Examine adoption\"| C[\"Subscriptions and actual use\"]\n  A -->|\"Examine operation\"| D[\"Service reachability\"]\n  D -->|\"Test separately\"| E[\"Domestic destinations\"]\n  D -->|\"Test separately\"| F[\"International destinations\"]\n```",
        "summary": "This editorial framework separates statistical measures from operational testing. The arrows identify assessment questions, not ownership or physical routing relationships. The cited technical study measures international reachability and does not directly test domestic connectivity.",
        "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "conclusion",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What a balanced account requires",
        "content": "A useful account of Iran's telecommunications must combine deployment evidence with information about use and the ability to reach required services. Aggregate access indicators and documented restrictions answer different questions, so neither should replace the other. The inspected evidence establishes this distinction, but further operator documentation, geographic detail, and annual statistical verification are needed before drawing current conclusions about service quality or competition. [s1][s2][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s4"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
